From 2d23c576df04611c2fc9758bd8b9c0663f3bb2f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 14:58:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 44/54] nouveau/vk: nvkmd_horizon, a wait may arrive before
 there is a fence

nvk_horizon_sync_wait sampled the sync's state once and returned
VK_TIMEOUT when nothing had been submitted with it -- instantly, and
including for OS_TIMEOUT_INFINITE.

The premise was right and the conclusion was wrong. Nothing this backend
does will signal a sync that has no fence, but this type advertises
CPU_WAIT and CPU_SIGNAL together, so another thread is permitted to
signal it or to submit with it while the first is inside
vkWaitForFences. Vulkan allows waiting on a fence no queue has touched
yet, and requires that wait to block until the timeout. Reporting a
timeout that has not happened turned a legal pattern into a spurious
failure.

The state is now shared properly rather than sampled: a mutex and a
condition variable in the sync, broadcast from signal(), set_fence() and
move() -- the three transitions that can release a waiter -- and not
from reset(), which makes the object less reachable and has nobody to
release. wait() blocks on the condvar until the sync acquires a fence,
then hands the remaining timeout to horizon_gpu as before.

Two details worth naming:

move() copies the whole payload struct, which would have copied the
destination's live mutex and condvar over with the source's -- including
the one held on that very line -- and then destroyed the wrong pair in
finish(). They are preserved across the copy like base is, and the two
objects are locked in address order so concurrent moves between the same
pair cannot deadlock.

The condvar wait is chunked at 100 ms rather than given the caller's
deadline, because cnd_timedwait takes an absolute TIME_UTC deadline and
on this platform that clock is the real-time clock (decision D8) -- the
exact thing this file converts away from at the top of every wait. A
chunk bounds how far a date change can perturb the wait; it costs no
wake-up latency, since a broadcast ends the chunk immediately.

The primitives are verified on this platform, not assumed: t_threads
exercises cnd_wait, cnd_signal, cnd_broadcast and cnd_timedwait against
their deadlines and passes 67/67 on console.

Decision D16, raised by review on PR #6.
